<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Mirdasid Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">1024 CE – 1080 CE · capital Aleppo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Bedouin dynasty of the Banu Kilab that ruled Aleppo for half a century, balancing between Byzantium and the Fatimids.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salih-ibn-mirdas">Salih ibn Mirdas</a>, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-kilab">Banu Kilab</a> who dominated the steppe of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>, joined the 1024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> from its Fatimid governor, becoming its emir.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/salihids">Salih</a> was killed in 1029 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-uqhuwana">al-Uqhuwana</a>, but his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibl-al-dawla-nasr">Shibl al-Dawla Nasr</a> defeated the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azaz">Azaz</a> in 1030 and then accepted Byzantine protection. The Mirdasids relied on tribal horsemen and the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shia-islam">Shi'i</a> notables, alternated between Fatimid and Byzantine alliances, and under Thimal ruled a wide area of northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>. Family quarrels repeatedly divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1070 Mahmud ibn Nasr switched allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> to placate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a>. Weakened by internal feuds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> pressure, the dynasty lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1080 to the Uqaylid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-ibn-quraysh">Muslim ibn Quraysh</a>, and the city soon passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/seljuk-sultanate-of-syria">Seljuk Sultanate of Syria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1024 CE – 1080 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:80.08%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1024 CE – 1080 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Aleppo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Salih ibn Mirdas, Shibl al-Dawla Nasr, Thimal ibn Salih</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mirdasids, Emirate of Aleppo (Mirdasid)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1052&amp;focus=mirdasid-emirate" aria-label="Mirdasid Emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5616 871 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mirdasid Emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5936 1127L5811 1337M5936 1127L5709 970M5936 1127L6481 1127M5936 1127L6085 1123"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5709 970h0M6481 1127h0M6085 1123h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks 